    "['match ip address prefix-list [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'from policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': 'from policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'from {', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': 'from {'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'prefix-list ALL;', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': 'prefix-list ALL;'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': '}', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interfaces [parameter2]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': '}'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'then accept;', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': 'then accept;'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': '}', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interfaces [parameter2]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `match ip address prefix-list ALL` command:\\n\\n##\\nfrom policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 {\\n    from {\\n        prefix-list ALL;\\n    }\\n    then accept;\\n}\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, prefix-list matching is typically done within a policy-statement term (equivalent to Cisco's route-map entry). The `then accept` is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's implicit `permit` in this context. The overall structure is different since Juniper handles routing policies differently than Cisco's route-maps.\", 'trans_template': '}'}]]": 1,
    "['set mpls-label', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'then accept', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"The equivalent Juniper command for Cisco's `set mpls-label` in a route-map context would be to use `then next-hop self` or `then install-nexthop` depending on the context, but for MPLS label pushing, the direct equivalent in a policy-statement would be:\\n\\n##\\nthen accept\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn't have a direct 1:1 command for `set mpls-label` in policy statements like Cisco. Instead, MPLS label handling is typically implicit in Juniper's MPLS forwarding when the protocol (like BGP-LU) is properly configured. The `then accept` is the closest equivalent since it allows the route to be installed in the routing table with its MPLS attributes.\\n\\nFor a more precise MPLS label operation in Juniper, you'd typically configure label-switched paths (LSPs) separately or use family mpls in protocols like BGP. The exact equivalent functionality would require broader context in Juniper's configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'then accept'}]]": 1,
    "['ip prefix-list [name] seq [sequence_number] [action] [network]/[prefix_length] le [max_prefix_length]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ip prefix-list` command:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `policy-options prefix-list` instead of `ip prefix-list`\\n- The sequence number (seq 5) is implicit in Juniper and doesn\\'t need to be specified\\n- `le 32` in Cisco translates to `upto /32` in Juniper\\n- The name \"ALL\" is preserved as the prefix-list name\\n- The permit action is implicit in Juniper\\'s prefix-list definition', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options prefix-list ALL 0.0.0.0/0 upto /32'}]]": 1
